        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.084,
            0.882,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "From Activity 12.2, we can explain that the population is a group of the same type of organisms in a habitat at a given time."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.123,
            0.883,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": "Can a habitat have only one type of living organism? What might happen then? If all organisms are the same, they would have the same requirements — food, water, space — leading to competition and possible scarcity of resources. What else do you think could happen?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.197,
            0.883,
            0.307
          ],
          "content": "In Activities 12.1 and 12.2, you observed that different group of organisms live together in a habitat. A **community** comprises different populations sharing the same habitat. The biotic components of a habitat, such as the plants, animals, and microorganisms together form the community. These organisms interact and depend on one another for survival."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.263,
            0.317,
            0.471,
            0.339
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Ever heard of ..."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.247,
            0.353,
            0.865,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "You may have seen brightly coloured flowers blooming around you. Have you ever looked closely at their parts? A flower has a stalk, green leafy structures called **sepals**, coloured **petals** and two reproductive"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.171,
            0.455,
            0.247,
            0.538
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.247,
            0.405,
            0.685,
            0.524
          ],
          "content": "parts — **Carpels** (female) and **stamens** (male). Stamens burst and release yellow dust like **pollen grains**. Wind, water, insects, bats, and birds help carry pollen from the stamens to the carpels of the same and/or different flowers. This process is called **pollination** (Fig. 12.2). It is essential for the formation of fruits and seeds."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.69,
            0.404,
            0.857,
            0.496
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.719,
                0.5,
                0.846,
                0.532
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 12.2: Insect pollination"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.274,
            0.553,
            0.857,
            0.603
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "12.3 Does Every Organism in a Community Matter?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.616,
            0.863,
            0.638
          ],
          "content": "Let us find out the role of different organisms in a community."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.654,
            0.597,
            0.677
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 12.3: Let us read"
        },
        {
